  "walkthroughs": [
    {
      "tag": "Two",
      "h2": "El & La",
      "body_html": "<p>Spanish has just two genders: <strong>el</strong> (masculine), <strong>la</strong> (feminine). 'El padre', 'la madre'. Most nouns ending in -o are masculine, -a are feminine.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Sibs",
      "h2": "Brothers & Sisters",
      "body_html": "<p><strong>El hermano</strong> = brother. <strong>La hermana</strong> = sister. Plurals add -s: <strong>los hermanos</strong>, <strong>las hermanas</strong>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Have",
      "h2": "Saying You Have",
      "body_html": "<p><strong>Tengo</strong> = I have. 'Tengo un hermano y dos hermanas' = 'I have one brother and two sisters'. <strong>Un/una</strong> = a/an.</p>"
    }
  ],
